  "summary": "Six preprints adapt protein language models to variant effects, PTMs, kcat, methyltransferases, remote homology and nitrogenase activity using task-specific inputs.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is specializing protein sequence representations for defined prediction tasks by adding task-relevant information rather than relying on generic embeddings. DynamicsPLM augments a 35M-parameter model with molecular dynamics heads for zero-shot variant effects. HieraPTM combines sequence, language model and 3D structure features to move from PTM site recognition to functional phosphosite prediction. EnzRef treats reactants and products separately against a frozen model for kcat. S-PLM-TM uses a structure-aware model for sequence-only remote homology search. SMSTPred applies embeddings to methyltransferase substrate specificity, and CarmnaDD predicts nitrogenase activity from sequence with dual descriptors (R2 0.60, AUC 0.94). The items are heterogeneous in task, but converge on compact, targeted adaptation.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-20"
}
